/* Pass 22A. Canon-exact Iroh + Love & Duty revisions. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-06":{"number":6,"title":"A Game of Pai Sho","content":"Iroh taught me pai sho by letting me lose.\n\n“You are not even pretending to be kind,” I said after my third defeat.\n\nHe gathered the tiles with a satisfied hum. “Kindness and strategy are not opposites.”\n\n“You moved three pieces I didn’t know existed.”\n\n“That is because you did not ask what they did.”\n\n“I assumed they were decorative.”\n\n“A common mistake.”\n\nThe tea shop was closed for the afternoon. Iroh had set up the game board on a low table near the window. Sunlight lay across the painted tiles. Outside, Ba Sing Se moved through its ordinary noise: carts, voices, distant bells.\n\nInside, the world had narrowed to red and white pieces, jasmine tea, and Iroh's hand resting beside mine.\n\n“What is the object of the game?” I asked.\n\n“To create harmony.”\n\n“You could have told me that before you destroyed me.”\n\n“Then you might have tried to create harmony too early.”\n\n“Is that bad?”\n\n“Harmony is not the same as peace. Sometimes the board must become complicated before it can become balanced.”\n\nI studied the pieces. “That sounds like something you say when you are winning.”\n\n“It is something I say because I am winning.”\n\nI threw a tile at him. It struck his shoulder and bounced into my lap.\n\nIroh laughed so loudly that a customer outside the shop turned to look through the window.\n\n“You are a dangerous player,” he said.\n\n“I’m an unconventional player.”\n\n“You are holding a flower tile upside down.”\n\n“Unconventional.”\n\nHe reached across the board and turned it for me. His fingers brushed mine.\n\nThe contact was brief, but it changed the room.\n\nIroh looked at my hand, then at my face. “You are distracted.”\n\n“You are sitting very close.”\n\n“The board is small.”\n\n“The table is large.”\n\n“An excellent observation.”\n\nI moved my hand away. He did not chase it.\n\n“Are you regretting last night?” I asked.\n\nIroh's expression softened. “No.”\n\n“You answer quickly.”\n\n“Some questions deserve a quick answer.”\n\n“And some?”\n\n“Some deserve tea first.”\n\nHe poured. I watched the steam rise between us.\n\n“Do you regret it?” he asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then we are in agreement.”\n\n“That was not the question I asked.”\n\n“You are right.”\n\nThe honesty warmed me more than the tea.\n\n“I’m not used to this,” I said.\n\n“To losing at pai sho?”\n\n“To someone being careful with me and still wanting me.”\n\nIroh's fingers tightened around his cup.\n\n“People often confuse desire with permission,” he said. “They believe wanting someone gives them the right to hurry.”\n\n“You don’t hurry.”\n\n“I have hurried before.”\n\n“You?”\n\n“I was young.”\n\n“You’re making yourself sound ancient.”\n\n“I am ancient compared to the tea leaves.”\n\n“That is not how age works.”\n\n“It is how tea works.”\n\nI smiled, but the question remained.\n\n“Why are you not hurrying now?” I asked.\n\nIroh set his cup down. “Because I would rather be certain you are walking toward me than discover you were only trying to keep up.”\n\nThe words went through me.\n\n“You make it very difficult to be casual.”\n\n“I have been accused of that.”\n\n“By whom?”\n\n“My nephew. He believes my habit of offering wisdom at breakfast is a form of attack.”\n\n“He may be right.”\n\n“He is often right. It is one of his more irritating qualities.”\n\nThe shop door opened. A messenger entered to deliver a bundle of tea. Iroh stood to sign for it. I watched the way he moved, broad and graceful, carrying a lifetime of discipline without letting it harden him.\n\nWhen he returned, he placed a small white tile beside my hand.\n\n“What does that do?” I asked.\n\n“It waits.”\n\n“That is a terrible game piece.”\n\n“Only if you believe waiting means doing nothing.”\n\n“You are going to say that patience is a strategy.”\n\n“Patience is a strategy.”\n\nI sighed. “I should have guessed.”\n\nHe taught me the names of the pieces. The white lotus. The red dragon. The wheel. The knot of destiny. Each tile carried a meaning, but none were absolute. A piece could support, obstruct, or transform depending on what surrounded it.\n\n“That sounds like people,” I said.\n\n“People are more complicated. They cannot be placed neatly on a board.”\n\n“You would be surprised what people try to do with me.”\n\nIroh looked at me. “I would not be surprised.”\n\nThe comment was so quiet I nearly missed it.\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“You have the posture of someone who has been assigned roles without being asked.”\n\nI looked down at the board.\n\n“The fixer,” I said. “The easy one. The person who leaves before being left.”\n\n“And who are you when nobody is assigning you a role?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Then we will not decide today.”\n\nHe moved the white lotus.\n\nBefore I could take my turn, a boy appeared at the doorway holding a broken wooden tile. He couldn't have been much older than twelve. The grain had split straight through a wheel.\n\n“My grandfather says you can tell where a board was made by the flower on the back,” he told Iroh. “Is that true?”\n\nIroh held out his hand, and the boy gave him the piece. He turned it over. Someone had scratched a crooked blossom into the wood.\n\n“It can tell you who made this tile,” he said. “Not necessarily where they learned to play.”\n\n“Grandfather says it came from a White Lotus master.”\n\n“Your grandfather may be right.” Iroh placed the halves together. “Would he mind if I asked him rather than guessing?”\n\nI watched the boy's disappointment settle into curiosity.\n\n“You don't know?”\n\n“I know more now than when you arrived,” Iroh said. “For example, I know the wood has split where someone tried to repair it with a nail. That is useful. And I know that the person who remembers its history is not standing in this room.”\n\nMina found a small cloth and wrapped the halves. She wrote the grandfather's address on a slip, correcting the boy when he left out the alley number. Iroh didn't announce he would restore the tile. He asked whether they wanted help finding a carpenter.\n\nOnce the boy left, I pointed at the lotus on our board.\n\n“Were you going to use that as a lesson about patience?”\n\n“I was considering it.”\n\n“That's unbearable.”\n\n“Then I am fortunate you haven't learned to defeat me yet.”\n\nHe pushed the tile toward me. It was my move, and for once he wasn't pretending my choice could be made for me.\n\n“Your turn.”\n\nI studied the board. The last game had been lost because I tried to protect one piece. This time, I let it go. I moved the wheel, then placed the lotus near the center.\n\nIroh's brows lifted.\n\n“Was that good?” I asked.\n\n“It was interesting.”\n\n“You say that when you don’t want to admit I’m winning.”\n\n“You are not winning.”\n\n“Yet.”\n\n“A dangerous word.”\n\nThe game lasted until the sun lowered. I did not win, but I forced Iroh to think. When he finally placed his last piece, he looked pleased rather than triumphant.\n\n“You have improved,” he said.\n\n“You sound surprised.”\n\n“I am delighted.”\n\nHe gathered the tiles. My hands met over the red dragon.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\n“Can I kiss you again?” I asked.\n\nIroh's smile was slow. “You may.”\n\nThe kiss began across the board. It was awkward because the table was between us, and Iroh laughed when I nearly knocked over the tea. I moved around the table. He rose to meet me.\n\nHis hands settled at my shoulders. When I stepped closer, they moved to my waist. The warmth of him gathered beneath my palms.\n\n“Still comfortable?” he murmured.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Still certain?”\n\n“Ask me tomorrow.”\n\nHe kissed me more deeply. His beard brushed my skin, soft and surprising. I smiled against his mouth.\n\n“What?” he asked.\n\n“You taste like jasmine.”\n\n“A professional hazard.”\n\n“A pleasant one.”\n\nThe shop's closing bell sounded even though the door was locked. Iroh pulled back with a sigh.\n\n“The kettle,” he said.\n\n“Your other lover?”\n\n“It is demanding.”\n\nHe went to check it, and I followed. The kiss remained between us, not finished, only paused.\n\nThat night, when I climbed the stairs to my room, a single white lotus tile rested on the windowsill.\n\nBeside it was a note in Iroh's careful handwriting: WAITING IS NOT DOING NOTHING.\n\nI smiled until I fell asleep.\n\nI woke before sunrise with the white lotus tile still in my hand.\n\nFor a moment, I did not remember where I was. Then the room returned: the slanted ceiling, the jasmine plant, the faint warmth rising through the floor from the kitchen below.\n\nThe note lay on the windowsill. I read it again.\n\nWAITING IS NOT DOING NOTHING.\n\nI carried the tile downstairs.\n\nIroh was already awake, standing over a kettle with an expression of deep suspicion.\n\n“It whistled at me,” he said.\n\n“Perhaps it wants breakfast.”\n\n“It wants authority.”\n\nI placed the tile on the counter. “You left this.”\n\n“I did.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you were trying to win by protecting every piece.”\n\n“That sounds like a criticism.”\n\n“It is an observation.”\n\n“You are very fond of observations.”\n\n“They are safer than predictions.”\n\nI looked at the tile. “What were you predicting?”\n\nIroh's hand went still on the kettle handle.\n\n“That you might decide last night was a mistake,” he said.\n\nThe honesty was quiet, without the armor of humor.\n\n“I didn’t,” I replied.\n\n“I am pleased.”\n\n“You could have asked.”\n\n“I could have.”\n\n“You are learning too.”\n\n“Every day.”\n\nI moved around the counter. He waited for me to close the distance.\n\n“The game is not the only place where I protect every piece,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“I want to stop.”\n\n“You may begin with one.”\n\nI touched his hand. “This one.”\n\nIroh's eyes warmed.\n\nHe kissed me in the quiet kitchen while the kettle simmered. There was no board between us this time. His arms held me close, but his body remained attentive to every change in mine.\n\nWhen the shop opened, I placed the white lotus tile beside the register.\n\n“Are you keeping it there?” he asked.\n\n“It is watching the game.”\n\n“It has a very important position.”\n\n“It is waiting.”\n\nIroh smiled. “Not doing nothing.”\n\nThe day began.\n\nI kept the white lotus tile near the register. Every time someone asked about it, Iroh answered that it was waiting for the right move.\n\nI never corrected him.\n\nThe next game lasted longer. I still lost, but Iroh had to think before claiming the final space.\n\nHe looked delighted.\n\n“You are enjoying my defeat,” I said.\n\n“I am enjoying your persistence.”\n\n“That is a nicer answer.”\n\n“It is also true.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nThe game waited.\n\nPatiently.\n\nAs I learned.","word_count":1792},"avatar-19":{"number":19,"title":"A Room Above the Tea Shop","content":"The room above the tea shop became mine one object at a time.\n\nThe third object was the hardest to explain.\n\nIt was a small brass key on a loop of red thread, the key to a room that did not belong to Iroh or this city. When I set it beside the bed he picked it up only after I offered it to him.\n\n“Another home?” he asked.\n\n“A door I can choose to open. Some days I need space to do work that has nothing to do with tea.”\n\nHe nodded, but his eyes went to the stairwell. I could see the shopkeeper in him calculating when someone would need an extra pair of hands.\n\n“I don't want a room upstairs to mean I'm on call whenever a customer wants something,” I said.\n\n“Of course not.”\n\n“Not even when the customer is you.”\n\nThat earned a laugh, brief and surprised.\n\n“I must confess I had hoped to monopolize your excellent judgment.”\n\n“You can request an appointment.”\n\n“Am I required to bring refreshments?”\n\n“Strongly encouraged.”\n\nHe set the key back where I had left it. That small act did more for the room than the curtains, the chest or the white lotus tile. He had lived long enough to make a habit of caring for people by arranging their lives. I wanted him close. I did not want our closeness to become a new command.\n\nWhen he leaned down to kiss my forehead, I caught his sleeve.\n\n“My mouth is down here.”\n\n“An excellent correction,” he murmured.\n\nHis beard grazed my cheek as he kissed me properly. From below, a pot clattered. We laughed into each other and stayed where we were until the kitchen had settled.\n\nI brought a small chest from the boarding house. Iroh carried it upstairs and refused to open it without permission.\n\n“It contains clothes,” I said.\n\n“Clothes are private.”\n\n“You have seen me wearing them.”\n\n“That is not the same as opening a chest.”\n\n“You are very principled for a man who hides pastries in his sleeves.”\n\n“The pastries are not private.”\n\nI unpacked while he stood by the window. The room had changed since I first saw it. A second chair sat beside the desk. A low shelf held my books. The jasmine plant had grown around the balcony rail.\n\n“Did you do this?” I asked.\n\n“The shelf was necessary.”\n\n“You built it.”\n\n“I assembled it.”\n\n“It is leaning.”\n\n“It has character.”\n\nI placed the last book on it. The shelf leaned more.\n\n“It has danger,” I said.\n\n“Danger and character are close companions.”\n\n“You are going to fix it.”\n\n“I am going to supervise you fixing it.”\n\nI handed him the hammer.\n\n“Very well,” he said.\n\nTogether, I repaired the shelf. He held the wood. I tightened the screws. When the shelf finally stood straight, Iroh looked pleased.\n\n“A miracle,” he said.\n\n“A screw.”\n\n“Miracles are often misunderstood.”\n\nI had heard the line before. This time, I kissed him before he could continue.\n\nThe decision to stay above the shop had not come from one conversation. It grew through many smaller ones. I discussed wages, privacy, days off, what would happen if I wanted a different job, and whether employees were allowed to date their employers.\n\n“The answer is complicated,” Iroh said.\n\n“The answer is boundaries.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“If we argue, we do not do it in front of customers.”\n\n“Agreed.”\n\n“If either of us needs space, we say so.”\n\n“Agreed.”\n\n“If I leave, you will not use the room or the job to make me stay.”\n\nIroh looked at me. “Agreed.”\n\n“If you leave, I will not use love to make you return.”\n\n“Agreed.”\n\nThe agreements were not unromantic. They gave the romance somewhere to stand.\n\nOn the first night after my things were unpacked, I and Iroh sat on the balcony. The city below glowed with lanterns. I shared a pot of tea and a plate of sesame pastries.\n\n“They are still shouting,” I said.\n\n“They have grown on you.”\n\n“Do not mistake tolerance for affection.”\n\n“I would never.”\n\nIroh leaned against the railing. His shoulder rested against mine.\n\n“What do you want this room to become?” he asked.\n\nI looked inside. The bed was neatly made. My clothes occupied one side of the chest. His books sat on a different shelf. The small white lotus tile from the first pai sho game rested beside the window.\n\n“A place where I can close the door without disappearing,” I said.\n\nIroh's expression softened.\n\n“A place where I can be with you without becoming part of you,” I continued. “And where you can come in without becoming responsible for everything I feel.”\n\n“That is a wise design.”\n\n“You’re not allowed to call it wise.”\n\n“Then I will call it ours.”\n\nThe word filled the balcony.\n\n“Ours,” I repeated.\n\nIroh took my hand. “If you want.”\n\n“I do.”\n\nHe kissed my knuckles. “Then ours.”\n\nI kissed him beneath the lantern light. His hand moved to my waist. The kiss grew deeper, but the evening remained gentle. There was no need to prove anything. I had already chosen.\n\nWhen I pulled back, Iroh touched my cheek.\n\n“May I stay?” he asked.\n\n“You live downstairs.”\n\n“That is not an answer.”\n\nI smiled. “Yes.”\n\nHe stayed.\n\nThe room above the shop held the sounds of the city, the kettle, and the two of us learning how to share a life without losing its separate edges.\n\nIn the morning, I woke before dawn and found his hand resting over my heart.\n\nThe position was not ownership.\n\nIt was listening.\n\nThe next week, I began looking for a location near the outer wall for the second tea room. Iroh carried a notebook. I carried a measuring cord and a list of practical questions.\n\n“How many tables?” I asked.\n\n“Enough for conversation.”\n\n“That is not a number.”\n\n“Eight.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“And a small stage.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Music. Stories. Perhaps pai sho tournaments.”\n\n“You want a tea shop or a community hall?”\n\n“A place where people can enter without needing to know what they are seeking.”\n\nI looked at him. “That sounds familiar.”\n\n“It is intentional.”\n\nThe building had once been a tailor shop. Its walls were cracked. The front windows were wide. A courtyard behind it held a tree that had survived neglect.\n\nI walked through the empty rooms while Iroh imagined shelves.\n\n“The kitchen goes here,” he said.\n\n“The counter there.”\n\n“A private room for you to rest.”\n\n“A storage room first.”\n\n“A private room for you to rest,” he repeated.\n\nI smiled. “You are still learning to prioritize.”\n\n“I am learning from your authority.”\n\nI measured the courtyard. “The tree needs pruning.”\n\n“It needs patience.”\n\n“It needs both.”\n\nHe stood behind me, looking at the branches.\n\n“What if we fail?” I asked.\n\n“Then we will have learned what the building wanted to become.”\n\n“Buildings do not want things.”\n\n“You have met very few buildings.”\n\nI leaned against him. “What if we succeed?”\n\n“Then we will need more tea.”\n\n“Your solutions are very consistent.”\n\n“Consistency is underrated.”\n\nRen helped repair the front door. The woman with the bright scarves chose fabric for the cushions. The old pai sho players argued about the placement of the game tables.\n\nThe tea room became a project shared by people who had not expected to share anything.\n\nOne evening, I found Iroh sitting alone in the courtyard with a letter from Zuko.\n\n“Bad news?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You look worried.”\n\n“He wants to visit.”\n\n“That is good.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“Why are you afraid?”\n\nIroh folded the letter. “Because he is my nephew. Because I love him. Because he may see what I have built and decide I have moved on without him.”\n\n“You have not.”\n\n“No. But love does not prevent misunderstanding.”\n\n“Then tell him.”\n\n“He may not believe me.”\n\n“Tell him again.”\n\nIroh looked at the unfinished courtyard. “You make it sound simple.”\n\n“It is not simple. It is just necessary.”\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n“You will be here when he comes?”\n\n“If you want me.”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Then I will.”\n\nThe tea room opened six months later. It was smaller than the Jasmine Dragon, but bright. The tree in the courtyard had been pruned and given new supports. The front windows carried the same dragon-and-cup sign, painted in different colors.\n\nOn opening day, Iroh gave me a key.\n\n“Another one?” I asked.\n\n“This one opens the courtyard gate.”\n\n“I already have keys to the shop, the room, and the back door.”\n\n“You are collecting them.”\n\n“You keep giving them to me.”\n\n“A sign of your excellent character.”\n\nI kissed him before the customers arrived.\n\nThe door opened.\n\nThe room filled with voices.\n\nI and Iroh stood behind the new counter, hand in hand beneath the table.\n\nThe future had a roof now.\n\nOn the first morning after opening, Zuko arrived before the doors. He stood outside with his hands in his coat pockets, staring at the painted sign.\n\nIroh opened the door.\n\n“You are early,” he said.\n\n“I could say the same.”\n\n“I own the shop.”\n\n“You have always been irritatingly prepared.”\n\nZuko looked at me. “You must be the one who keeps him from working himself into the ground.”\n\n“I am trying.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nIroh looked between us. “I am standing here.”\n\n“We know,” Zuko said.\n\nI served them tea in the courtyard. Zuko tasted it and frowned.\n\n“It is good,” he said.\n\n“You sound disappointed,” Iroh replied.\n\n“I expected it to be worse.”\n\n“A high compliment.”\n\nThe three of us talked until the sun reached the top of the tree. Zuko asked about the road. Iroh answered. I filled in the parts he left out: the storm, the flowers, the child with the turtleduck question.\n\nZuko listened with a softness he did not show often.\n\nWhen he left, Iroh stood beneath the sign.\n\n“You were worried,” I said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It went well.”\n\n“It did.”\n\n“You are allowed to enjoy that.”\n\nHe looked toward the courtyard. “I am learning.”\n\nI took his hand. “Then keep learning.”\n\nThe second tea room filled with sunlight and voices.\n\nIroh stood beside me at the counter, watching the new place become alive. His thumb brushed my knuckles beneath the table.\n\n“Still with me?” he asked.\n\n“Still with you.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it beneath the new counter.\n\nThe gesture was small, but the future felt large around it.\n\nTogether, I opened the front door.\n\nThe courtyard waited for its first morning.\n\nThe tree held the lanterns above it.\n\nI stood beneath them with Iroh's hand in mine, watching the new room become a place.\n\nIt was a good place.","word_count":1778},"avatar-36":{"number":36,"title":"A Promise with No Audience","content":"The balcony had one good chair and two people who wanted it.\n\nIroh discovered this at sunset and behaved as though we had reached an international crisis.\n\n\"I insist you sit,\" he said.\n\n\"I insist we stop pretending this chair is a throne.\"\n\n\"It has excellent support.\"\n\n\"It has a splinter.\"\n\nHe looked at the seat, then at me.\n\n\"An imperfection in the kingdom.\"\n\nI laughed and sat on the low bench beside the railing instead. It was cooler there. The city had quieted after the tea rush, though I could still hear the last delivery cart turning into the lane.\n\nIroh joined me with two cups.\n\nWe had planned a private evening. No festival, no lesson, no public speech. He had asked whether we could talk about what we wanted next, and I'd agreed before either of us could invent a reason to put it off.\n\nHe set his cup down carefully.\n\n\"Are you nervous?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\n\"About talking to me?\"\n\n\"About speaking as though I know the future.\"\n\n\"You don't have to.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"I suppose I was hoping for an impressive answer.\"\n\n\"I've already heard your impressive answers. I'd like a true one.\"\n\nThe balcony went quiet.\n\nI could smell the evening's last jasmine steeping downstairs. The shutters had been repaired that morning. Someone had replaced a loose nail in the rail and left the spare on the sill, where Iroh kept moving it out of sight and forgetting to put it away.\n\n\"I want you to come back,\" he said at last.\n\n\"I plan to.\"\n\n\"That was not a demand. I know you have other people you love.\"\n\n\"I know you know.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"I sometimes feel foolish for wanting a promise when we've already spoken plainly.\"\n\n\"Wanting reassurance isn't foolish. What kind of promise?\"\n\nHe looked toward the lane.\n\n\"A morning. Perhaps Thursday. The kind of ordinary plan we can keep even when the rest of our worlds insist on being complicated.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Thursday morning I have to be at Moonroot.\"\n\nHis face fell before he could stop it.\n\n\"Friday?\"\n\nI checked the small calendar tucked in my notebook.\n\n\"Friday works. I can come through the Threadway in the afternoon and stay for breakfast the next day.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"You can simply choose that?\"\n\n\"Yes. So can you. You can tell me if you need to change it.\"\n\nWe had been explicit about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. It had never meant I could offer everybody every hour. It meant the opposite: telling the truth about the hours I actually had, letting people decide whether they wanted to share them, and hearing their disappointment without trying to make it disappear.\n\nIroh leaned against the rail.\n\n\"And what if I have an errand for Zuko that takes longer than expected?\"\n\n\"Tell me. We'll choose another day. I might be disappointed, but that doesn't mean you've failed a test.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"What if I would rather not entertain the Fire Lord's next messenger at all?\"\n\n\"Then you can say the shop is closed and reply tomorrow.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"I may have to practice that sentence.\"\n\n\"Write it down.\"\n\nHe pulled a scrap of paper from his sleeve and wrote: *I will answer tomorrow.*\n\nI stared.\n\n\"Do you carry blank paper for every possible piece of advice?\"\n\n\"Only the important ones.\"\n\nI took the pencil and added: *Unless the kitchen is on fire.*\n\n\"An excellent amendment,\" he said solemnly.\n\nMina knocked at the balcony door just then.\n\nBefore she knocked, Iroh took the small calendar from the window ledge.\n\n\"You mentioned Moonroot,\" he said. \"Will the archive need you the whole morning?\"\n\n\"Probably. Orin has a public records session, and they've asked whether I can help visitors find the right evidence without bringing private files into the reading room.\"\n\n\"Will Severus be there?\"\n\n\"Yes. He'll be checking the citations.\"\n\nIroh smiled faintly.\n\n\"A formidable taskmaster.\"\n\n\"Only if you try to cite a teapot as a historical witness.\"\n\n\"An oversight I shall avoid.\"\n\nI hesitated.\n\n\"Does it bother you, hearing about the people I love?\"\n\nThe question had been sitting between us since I'd set the calendar on the balcony rail. One Friday morning was easy to promise. A whole life with more than one person mattered too much to squeeze into a single square.\n\nIroh took his time answering.\n\n“I have spent years teaching young people not to believe love is a contest,” he said. “It would be unfortunate if I began keeping score now.”\n\n“You're allowed to feel jealous.”\n\n“Indeed. I am less fond of the feeling than I expected.”\n\nHe glanced at my notebook, where the word *Moonroot* was written beside Severus's name.\n\n“I envy his ability to share your work,” Iroh admitted. “Not because he possesses something that should be mine, but because he sees a part of you I can only ask about.”\n\n“That doesn't stop you from asking.”\n\n“I know.” He rubbed his thumb along the edge of the cup. “And I must remember that asking is not the same as requiring an answer.”\n\nI moved close enough that our shoulders touched. He did not convert his fear into a lesson. That made it easier for me to say the next thing.\n\n“I am ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. That means I want each relationship to be something we choose with care, not a promise to ration myself until nobody feels anything difficult.”\n\nHe turned toward me. “Then let us begin by keeping Friday. And by telling the truth if Friday becomes difficult.”\n\nHis hand found mine under the edge of the bench. I kissed him there, behind the little screen of leaves, while the city continued without paying us the slightest attention.\n\nHe considered the question instead of giving me an easy answer.\n\n\"Sometimes it reminds me that your world is larger than the days I see. That can make me lonely. But I would be lonelier if you had to become a stranger in order to visit me.\"\n\nI let that settle.\n\n\"I don't want to make you listen to details you haven't asked for. But I do want to share the part of my life that affects our time together.\"\n\n\"Then tell me what changes the calendar, and tell me the stories you actually wish to tell. We can ask each other when a subject is difficult.\"\n\nThe wind made the torn corner of the calendar flutter.\n\n\"That sounds possible,\" I said.\n\nHe reached toward the loose sheet.\n\n\"Perhaps we should fasten this before our carefully negotiated Friday escapes into the street.\"\n\nI found the little brass clip in the drawer, and we secured the calendar. It was absurd how comforting a small piece of metal could be.\n\n\"I heard that,\" she said. \"The kitchen is not on fire. But the storage shelf still needs a carpenter's estimate. Shall I call one tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Please,\" Iroh answered. \"And do not give them my personal number for evening visits.\"\n\nMina lifted an eyebrow.\n\n\"Progress.\"\n\nWhen she had gone, Iroh looked pleased with himself.\n\n\"I have made a boundary.\"\n\n\"Very impressive.\"\n\n\"I should have the chair repaired in celebration.\"\n\n\"After you get the estimate.\"\n\nHe groaned theatrically.\n\nThe wind turned colder. He took the extra shawl from the bench and held it toward me.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe set it around my shoulders without treating it as a reason to pull me into his lap. I leaned into him a moment later because I wanted to.\n\n\"What do you want from us?\" he asked.\n\nI took longer to answer than I'd expected.\n\n\"Not just comfort,\" I said. \"I want to be able to disagree with you. To laugh when your advice is terrible. To go home to another person without you pretending that makes this less real.\"\n\nHis hand tightened once, then relaxed.\n\n\"And I want to be told when a plan changes,\" he said. \"Not every private detail. Just what affects the time we have chosen.\"\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"We make very unpoetic promises.\"\n\n\"Good. Poetry makes terrible calendars.\"\n\nWe didn't kiss immediately.\n\nIroh paused when I leaned toward him, a faint crease between his brows.\n\n\"What is it?\" I asked.\n\n\"I was wondering whether we have made the evening sound like a business arrangement.\"\n\nI looked at the calendar, the repaired chair, and the note about not answering messengers after closing.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"That was not entirely my intention.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"I want the ordinary work with you,\" he said. \"But I also want the part where we are allowed to be foolish. I do not wish to court you with a ledger.\"\n\n\"Good, because your handwriting would frighten me.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"Then ask me for something that isn't on the calendar.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Would you dance with me here, even if there is no music and the balcony is quite small?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe moved carefully between the bench and the railing, laughing when his robe caught the chair leg. He steadied himself without trying to make the stumble invisible. I kept my hand in his because I wanted to.\n\nOnly when the wind grew cold did we stop. He rested his forehead against mine.\n\n\"Now?\" he asked.\n\n\"Now.\"\n\nWe kissed at the balcony door, slowly enough that I could feel him smile before I pulled back. The warmth between us didn't require an audience or a vow no one could revise.\n\nHe asked whether I'd like to stay the night. I said yes. We put the cups away, checked the shutters, and closed the door.\n\nOn Friday, a small card waited beside the breakfast table: *Reserved for two people who asked.*\n\nUnderneath it, in smaller handwriting, Mina had added: *Please return the chair to the carpenter.*\n\nI laughed so hard I had to sit down.\n\nIroh poured the tea and looked thoroughly delighted.","word_count":1650}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-06":{"number":6,"title":"What the Ground Knows","content":"Toph decided I needed self-defense lessons because I lost an argument with a crate.\n\nThe crate had been falling from a loading platform.\n\nThe following morning Toph marched me back to the same loading platform. A worker was there inspecting a frayed rope, and the entire crate had been lowered onto the ground.\n\n“See?” Toph said. “Not a mysterious attack by boxes. Somebody tied a bad knot.”\n\nThe worker looked defensive. “I didn't tie that one.”\n\n“Didn't say you did. I'm saying we're fixing the problem before Paperwork here practices being crushed again.”\n\nI wanted to be offended by the nickname. Instead I watched her find the loose end with her fingers, test the rope's tension, and call the foreman over to explain where the platform needed a second stop.\n\n“You're not teaching me to fight crates,” I said.\n\n“Congratulations. You're learning to notice when they're about to become somebody else's problem.”\n\nThe foreman promised to check the rest of the hoist. Toph asked him to put the result on the job board and tell the next shift, not just us. She wasn't asking for a medal; she wanted to know no one would stand beneath an unsafe load after we went home.\n\nWhen we reached the yard she picked up a padded staff.\n\n“Now,” she said, “about that self-defense lesson. I'm going to be right here. You can tell me to stop whenever you need.”\n\n“Does that make the next half-hour less terrifying?”\n\n“Nope.” She grinned. “But it makes it yours.”\n\nI had been standing under it.\n\nI moved in time, which I considered a victory.\n\nToph considered the entire incident a personal insult.\n\n“You froze.”\n\n“For half a second.”\n\n“That’s enough time to get flattened.”\n\n“I did not get flattened.”\n\n“Because I was there.”\n\n“You’re very humble.”\n\n“I’m the greatest earthbender in the world. Humility would be lying.”\n\nThat was how I found myself barefoot in the courtyard behind her metalbending school at dawn, regretting every choice that had led me there.\n\n“I’m not an earthbender,” I reminded her.\n\n“No kidding.”\n\n“I cannot feel the ground the way you do.”\n\n“Also noticed.”\n\n“What, exactly, are you teaching me?”\n\nToph planted one heel and folded her arms. “How to stop acting like being a nonbender means your only choices are run or wait for somebody shinier to save you.”\n\nThe words hit harder than the crate would have.\n\nI stared at her.\n\nToph’s expression did not change.\n\n“Too mean?” she asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nShe tossed me a padded practice staff.\n\nThe first lesson was not fighting.\n\nIt was balance.\n\nToph made me stand with my knees bent while she shoved my shoulders from different angles. Every time I stumbled, she called me a decorative fence post. Every time I corrected, she said nothing—which I quickly learned was praise.\n\n“Stop trying to guess where I’m going,” she said.\n\n“I’d love to. You’re hitting me.”\n\n“I’m barely touching you.”\n\n“You throw rocks for fun. Your scale is broken.”\n\nA grin flashed across her face.\n\nShe came at me again.\n\nThis time I did not try to outthink her. I felt the pressure through my feet, the change in my center, the direction of her hand. I stepped instead of locking my knees.\n\nToph’s palm slid past my shoulder.\n\n“There.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“That was good?”\n\n“Don’t ruin it.”\n\nWe worked until sweat dampened my shirt and the city began waking beyond the school walls.\n\nToph moved through the courtyard as if every inch belonged to her because, in a way, it did. She knew where I stood from the tremor of my weight. She knew when a student entered the far gate. She knew when a cart rolled down the street outside.\n\nShe also knew when I watched her.\n\n“Again,” she said.\n\n“I need water.”\n\n“You need to stop staring at my arms.”\n\nI nearly dropped the staff.\n\nToph laughed so hard she had to brace a hand on her knee.\n\n“I was not—”\n\n“Your heartbeat did the thing.”\n\n“My heartbeat is none of your business.”\n\n“It’s stomping on my business.”\n\nI threw the padded staff at her.\n\nShe caught it.\n\nOf course she did.\n\n“Violence,” she said approvingly. “We’re making progress.”\n\nI sat on the courtyard step and drank from my flask.\n\nToph dropped down beside me.\n\nFor a few minutes, we listened to the sounds of the school. Metal doors. Student voices. Someone swearing after apparently bending the wrong piece of railing.\n\n“You built this fast,” I said.\n\n“People wanted to learn.”\n\n“That isn’t what I meant.”\n\nToph tilted her head.\n\nI looked across the courtyard. “You made a place where nobody gets told their way of sensing the world is wrong.”\n\nHer face went still.\n\nI immediately worried I had stepped somewhere private.\n\nThen she picked at a chip in the stone beside her foot.\n\n“My parents had rooms for everything,” she said. “Music. Tea. Guests. Lessons. Rooms nobody used because they were supposed to look nice.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“They didn’t have a room for me to be good at anything they didn’t approve of.”\n\nThere was no self-pity in the words. Toph hated pity too much to carry it openly. There was anger, old and polished smooth from years of handling.\n\n“So you built one,” I said.\n\n“I built a lot.”\n\n“You did.”\n\nHer shoulder pressed against mine.\n\nNot an accident.\n\n“Don’t get weird,” she said.\n\n“I wasn’t.”\n\n“You were about to.”\n\n“Your ability is ruining my emotional privacy.”\n\n“Your emotional privacy is loud.”\n\nI smiled into my water flask.\n\nToph’s voice changed. Softer, though she would have denied it under oath.\n\n“You don’t treat me like I’m fragile.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You also don’t do that thing where people get so scared of treating me like I’m fragile that they refuse to help with anything.”\n\nI turned toward her.\n\nToph kept her face angled toward the courtyard.\n\n“That sounds exhausting,” I said.\n\n“It is.”\n\n“What do you want me to do?”\n\n“Ask.”\n\nThe answer came immediately.\n\n“Just ask?”\n\n“Yeah. If I need an arm because we’re on ice, I’ll say yes. If I don’t, I’ll tell you to get lost.”\n\n“That second part sounds familiar.”\n\n“Good. You’re learning.”\n\nWe sat there with our shoulders touching.\n\nI thought about all the ways people tried to turn care into guessing. Guessing what someone wanted. Guessing what help meant. Guessing whether love made permission unnecessary.\n\nToph, for all her bluntness, seemed to hate guessing more than anyone.\n\n“Can I ask you something?” I said.\n\n“You just did.”\n\n“Toph.”\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Does it bother you when I’m interested in Aang?”\n\nShe did not move.\n\nMy pulse betrayed me before my courage could.\n\n“And Katara,” I added.\n\nToph’s mouth curved.\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“The actual question.”\n\nI waited.\n\nShe tapped two fingers against the stone between us.\n\n“I’ve known those idiots a long time,” she said. “Aang loves like he’s opening every window in a house. Katara loves like she’s building the house around you and then getting mad when you don’t wear a coat.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nToph’s grin softened.\n\n“They’re not things I own.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“And neither are you.”\n\nMy breath caught.\n\nHer head turned toward me.\n\n“You thought I didn’t know?”\n\n“I don’t know what you know.”\n\n“I know when you lie. I know when Aang walks into a room and your heartbeat gets stupid. I know when Katara touches your arm and it gets stupider.”\n\nI covered my face.\n\n“And,” she continued mercilessly, “I know it does the same thing when I get close.”\n\nThe courtyard became very quiet.\n\nI lowered my hands.\n\nToph was smiling, but not like she had won.\n\nNot exactly.\n\n“Does that bother you?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer landed with startling force.\n\n“Does it scare you?”\n\nHer smile disappeared.\n\nThere was the vulnerable edge she usually buried under jokes.\n\n“A little,” she said.\n\nI did not rush to reassure her.\n\nThat would have made it smaller than it was.\n\n“What part?”\n\n“The part where people decide closeness means they get to decide things for me.”\n\nI nodded.\n\n“And the part where I might actually want something enough to be mad if it goes away.”\n\nThat one hurt differently.\n\nI set my hand on the stone between us, palm up.\n\nNot touching.\n\nAn offer.\n\nToph was blind. She could not see the gesture.\n\nBut the shift of my arm moved through the ground.\n\nHer hand found mine.\n\nShe laced our fingers together like it annoyed her.\n\n“You’re still terrible at stance,” she said.\n\n“I assumed this meant class was over.”\n\n“It means I’m giving you thirty seconds.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“To get sappy in your head.”\n\nI squeezed her hand.\n\nToph squeezed back.\n\nThirty seconds became a minute.\n\nNeither of us mentioned it.\n\nWhen we stood, she attacked without warning.\n\nI stepped sideways instead of freezing.\n\nHer hand missed my shoulder.\n\nToph’s grin was fierce and bright.\n\n“Again.”\n\nThis time, I was ready.\n\nThe lesson ended only when my arms started shaking badly enough that even Toph admitted the useful part was over.\n\nShe sat beside me on a sun-warmed slab of stone while I tried to recover what dignity remained.\n\n“You know,” I said, “most teachers say things like good effort.”\n\n“Most teachers are cowards.”\n\n“You could try encouragement.”\n\n“I encouraged you not to get flattened.”\n\n“That was a threat.”\n\n“Motivating, though.”\n\nI laughed, then winced when every muscle in my shoulders objected.\n\nToph’s expression shifted toward concern and immediately disguised itself as annoyance.\n\n“You’re going to be sore.”\n\n“I gathered that.”\n\n“Hot bath. Stretch. Don’t let Sugar Queen heal every ache just because she can. Your body should learn what it did.”\n\nThere was a philosophy buried inside the insult, as usual.\n\nI looked across the practice yard. “Was Aang really this bad?”\n\n“Worse.”\n\nAang, approaching with a basket of lunch, called, “I can hear you!”\n\n“I know!”\n\nHe sat on my other side. “She made me punch rocks.”\n\n“You were being evasive.”\n\n“I was twelve.”\n\n“Tiny evasive.”\n\nAang handed me a bun. “For the record, you did great.”\n\nToph made a gagging sound.\n\nI took the food. “Thank you.”\n\n“Traitor,” Toph said.\n\nBut when we stood, she slowed her stride to mine.\n\nNot enough to acknowledge it.\n\nEnough that I noticed.\n\nThe ground had a language. So did Toph. Both required paying attention to what happened underneath the obvious thing.\n\n“Again,” Toph said, and this time I laughed before I moved.\n","word_count":1739},"Love-And-Duty-19":{"number":19,"title":"The Wrong Kind of Silence","content":"I became unbearable so gradually that I almost missed it.\n\nAt first, I was simply busy.\n\nThe council was preparing a new housing survey. The eastern clinic needed supply allocations. Toph’s academy wanted permits for an expanded training yard. Aang’s Air Nomad school had acquired land but no legal category because Cranefish Town had never needed a form for “religious-cultural educational sanctuary with large flying mammals.”\n\nI created one.\n\nThe form came back from the clerk marked *incomplete*. The problem wasn't the name of the school. It was the box marked *responsible person*. I had put Aang's name there because he was the Avatar, and everybody was accustomed to watching him take responsibility for a room the moment he entered it.\n\nWhen I showed him the page, he read it and frowned.\n\n“I can sign it,” he said.\n\n“I know. But should you be the person they call when a sink breaks?”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n“The school is part of keeping my people alive.”\n\n“So is teaching somebody else how to keep it open when you're away.”\n\nKatara came through the kitchen with a stack of clean clinic towels. “Who actually manages the building?”\n\nI opened my mouth and discovered I didn't know. Toph, passing behind us, made a sound of pure satisfaction.\n\n“You made a form for a job nobody agreed to do,” she said. “Congratulations, Paperwork.”\n\nIt hurt because she was right. I had been so afraid of an important project going undone that I had quietly turned it into a responsibility shared by nobody but me and the man I loved.\n\nWe brought the question to the school's teachers. One agreed to handle building requests for a paid afternoon each week; another would maintain the lesson calendar, provided nobody assumed that meant teaching every class. Aang signed as founder, not permanent caretaker of every pipe and hinge.\n\nI took the amended form back to the clerk. It was accepted. I should have been happy.\n\nInstead I sat outside the office and cried from sheer exhaustion. Having one fewer thing to fix hadn't yet taught my body how to stop bracing for it.\n\nThat should have warned me.\n\nInstead I started waking before the others so I could work in the quiet room at the back of the house.\n\nThen I began bringing papers to breakfast.\n\nThen dinner.\n\nThen bed, once, until Katara removed them from my lap and said, “Absolutely not.”\n\n“I’m almost done.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“It’s one page.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Half a page.”\n\nToph, already under the blankets, said, “I support Sugar Queen’s dictatorship.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nI put the papers away.\n\nThen I waited until everyone slept and got them back out.\n\nThree nights later, I snapped at Aang for humming.\n\nHe stopped immediately.\n\nThe silence that followed was worse than the humming had been.\n\n“I’m sorry,” I said.\n\n“It’s okay.”\n\nIt was not.\n\nThe next morning I apologized again by making tea.\n\nThe morning after that, I forgot I had agreed to meet Katara for lunch.\n\nShe waited forty minutes.\n\nWhen I finally arrived at the clinic, breathless and carrying three folders, she was sitting on the steps with two untouched bowls beside her.\n\n“Oh.”\n\nThe sound came out small.\n\nKatara looked at me.\n\nShe did not yell.\n\nThat was worse too.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I lost track of time.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I can still—”\n\n“No.”\n\nHer voice was gentle.\n\nFirm.\n\nI stopped.\n\nKatara stood and handed one bowl to a passing medic.\n\n“Come home when you’re done.”\n\nThen she went back inside.\n\nI spent the afternoon furious.\n\nNot at her.\n\nThat would have been easier.\n\nAt myself for disappointing her. At the council for needing everything. At the city for growing problems faster than we could solve them. At every person who had told me I was useful until usefulness became the closest thing I had to proof that I deserved to stay.\n\nI worked later.\n\nOf course I did.\n\nWhen I got home, the house was quiet.\n\nToph sat at the kitchen table.\n\nShe did not look up.\n\n“You’re lying.”\n\nI put down my bag. “I haven’t said anything.”\n\n“Your whole body is lying.”\n\n“That’s not how—”\n\n“Don’t explain seismic sense to me, Paperwork.”\n\nI shut my mouth.\n\nToph tapped one finger against the table.\n\n“You walk different when you’re hiding something.”\n\n“I’m tired.”\n\n“Yeah.”\n\n“That’s it.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI hated how quickly the word landed.\n\n“Where are Aang and Katara?”\n\n“Roof.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because Katara wanted to yell at you and Aang told her to wait until you ate.”\n\nMy stomach dropped.\n\nToph’s mouth twitched.\n\n“That was a joke.”\n\n“Not funny.”\n\n“Little funny.”\n\nShe pushed a bowl toward me.\n\nI sat.\n\nThe food was warm.\n\nThat meant somebody had reheated it recently.\n\nMy throat tightened for reasons that made me angry.\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\nToph’s expression hardened.\n\n“Do you think I’m stupid?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then stop giving me stupid answers.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nNo gentle invitation. No careful opening.\n\nToph had spent her childhood trapped behind people deciding what she could handle. She considered dishonesty a kind of locked door.\n\nI stared at the soup.\n\n“If I stop,” I said, “things fall through.”\n\n“Some things.”\n\n“Important things.”\n\n“Sure.”\n\n“People could get hurt.”\n\nToph leaned back.\n\n“And if you don’t stop?”\n\nI did not answer.\n\nShe listened to my silence.\n\n“People still get hurt,” she said.\n\nThat was cruel.\n\nIt was also accurate.\n\nKatara had waited with two bowls.\n\nAang had stopped humming because my nerves had turned his joy into noise.\n\nToph had been sitting alone in the kitchen waiting for my footsteps.\n\nI rubbed both hands over my face.\n\n“I don’t know how to be needed a normal amount.”\n\nThe confession escaped before I could stop it.\n\nToph went quiet.\n\nThen she stood.\n\nFor one awful second I thought she was leaving.\n\nInstead she came around the table and sat on the floor beside my chair, back against my shin.\n\nNot looking at me.\n\nNot making me look at her.\n\n“Yeah,” she said.\n\nOne word.\n\nUnderstanding without pretending we had the same wound.\n\nI ate the soup.\n\nWhen the bowl was empty, Toph shouted, “You can come down now!”\n\nAang’s voice answered from the roof. “We weren’t listening!”\n\n“You’re a terrible liar!”\n\nKatara came downstairs first.\n\nHer anger was still there.\n\nGood.\n\nI did not want it erased because I finally admitted I was struggling.\n\nShe sat across from me.\n\n“I was hurt.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Don’t say it like that ends the sentence.”\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“I was hurt that you forgot me. And I’m scared because you’re treating exhaustion like evidence you’re doing enough.”\n\nAang sat beside her.\n\nI looked at the three of them.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\nKatara nodded once. “Thank you.”\n\nI waited for forgiveness.\n\nShe did not hand it over immediately.\n\nThat was another thing I loved about her. Katara believed in compassion, but she did not confuse compassion with pretending harm had not happened.\n\n“What do you need?” Aang asked.\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“That can be true,” he said. “But it can’t be the end of the conversation forever.”\n\nToph tilted her head back against my knee. “Look at Twinkle Toes growing a spine.”\n\n“I’ve had one.”\n\n“You borrowed mine.”\n\nKatara almost smiled.\n\nI looked toward the hallway leading to my office.\n\nThe door was open.\n\nPapers covered the desk.\n\nFor weeks I had treated that room as proof I had boundaries while carrying work out of it constantly.\n\n“I need the door rule back,” I said.\n\nKatara frowned. “The door rule?”\n\n“When I leave that room, work stays there unless there’s an actual emergency.”\n\nToph raised a hand. “Define emergency.”\n\n“Fire. Flood. Building collapse. Active violence. Somebody will lose food, shelter, medicine, or legal protection before morning if I do nothing.”\n\n“No flags?” Aang asked.\n\n“No flags.”\n\n“Even ugly ones?”\n\n“Especially ugly ones.”\n\nToph nodded solemnly. “Brave.”\n\nKatara said, “One day off each week.”\n\nI opened my mouth.\n\nShe stared at me.\n\n“One day,” I agreed.\n\nAang added, “And if you miss plans, you tell the person why before you start apologizing. Not so we can excuse it. So you don’t hide behind ‘busy.’”\n\nThat one hurt.\n\nWhich meant it belonged in the agreement.\n\nI nodded.\n\nKatara’s shoulders lowered slightly.\n\n“I’m still mad.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But I don’t want to punish you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Stop saying that.”\n\n“Sorry.”\n\nToph snorted.\n\nKatara’s mouth betrayed her with the beginning of a smile.\n\nI reached across the table, palm up.\n\nShe looked at my hand.\n\nThen took it.\n\nNot everything repaired in one gesture.\n\nThe next week, I forgot myself twice.\n\nThe first time, Toph walked into my office at dinner and rang Aang’s ceremonial bell beside my ear.\n\nI screamed.\n\nThe second time, Katara simply stood in the doorway holding my coat.\n\nI looked at the unfinished report.\n\nThen at her.\n\nI closed the folder.\n\nWe walked to a noodle stall three streets away.\n\nAang met us there after a meeting. Toph arrived ten minutes later with two students and no explanation for why one had blue paint in his hair.\n\nNobody asked me about the report.\n\nFor the first half hour, I kept thinking about it anyway.\n\nThen Aang challenged Toph to balance noodles on chopsticks using only airbending and seismic sense, which was exactly as stupid as it sounded.\n\nKatara laughed into my shoulder.\n\nMy body, traitor that it was, began to unclench.\n\nSilence had many shapes.\n\nThere was the good kind: four people reading in the same room, the scratch of pen and whisper of turning pages, nobody demanding anything.\n\nThere was the sacred kind: Aang meditating, Katara healing, Toph listening to the earth.\n\nAnd there was the wrong kind.\n\nThe silence made when I swallowed every need until the people who loved me had to guess what was happening from the damage around it.\n\nI was trying to learn the difference.\n\nSlowly.\n\nLoudly, according to Toph.\n\nBut learning.\n\nThe repair began with one rule: I was not allowed to apologize for resting.\n\nThis proved harder than resting itself.\n\nEvery time I woke from a nap and saw someone else working, guilt climbed into my throat before I could stop it. Katara caught me once halfway through saying sorry and held up one finger.\n\n“No.”\n\n“But—”\n\n“No.”\n\nToph, from the floor, said, “If you apologize again, I’m charging you.”\n\nAang looked up from his papers. “Can we actually do that?”\n\n“No,” I said.\n\n“Shame.”\n\nThe joke did not cure me.\n\nIt gave me enough room to practice.\n\nEventually, rest stopped feeling like stolen time. Not always. Enough that I could close my eyes without first earning permission from an imaginary judge.\n\nSome nights I still apologized. The difference was that now I heard myself doing it, and sometimes I could stop halfway through.\n","word_count":1793},"Love-And-Duty-36":{"number":36,"title":"A Future We Choose","content":"For three months after the conversation about children, nothing happened.\n\nThis was exactly what we had agreed to.\n\nIt still surprised me.\n\nNo secret planning. No Aang quietly collecting Air Nomad baby names. No Katara staring wistfully at infants and turning that into a referendum. No Toph pretending to hate every child she met while building them increasingly elaborate metal toys.\n\nActually, Toph did that last one.\n\nBut she had done it before.\n\nThe future stayed where it belonged: ahead of us.\n\nThen a girl named Mei arrived at the cultural school with a stolen satchel, two bruised knees, and no intention of telling anyone where she lived.\n\nShe was fourteen.\n\nNonbender.\n\nFire Nation father, Earth Kingdom mother, both dead in the storm.\n\nShe had been staying with an uncle who worked nights at the rail yard until he was injured and sent to a charity ward. Mei decided, with the brutal arithmetic of children who have learned not to trust systems, that disappearing would create fewer expenses.\n\nAang found her asleep in the archive room.\n\nToph found the hidden knife in her boot.\n\nKatara found the infected cut on her knee.\n\nI found the forms.\n\nOf course I did.\n\nTemporary guardianship required signatures from three offices, two witnesses, and a district magistrate who had gone to Ba Sing Se for six weeks.\n\n“Why?” Toph demanded.\n\n“Because the law assumes children only become homeless during business hours.”\n\n“Break it.”\n\n“The law?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nAang rubbed his forehead. “Maybe don’t say that in front of the clerk.”\n\nThe clerk, sitting three feet away, said, “Please don’t.”\n\nWe did not bring Mei home immediately.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nThe school had supervised dormitories. She chose a room there. Katara treated her knee. Aang asked permission before contacting the uncle. Toph explained every step of the process in blunt language that made the social workers wince and Mei trust her almost instantly.\n\n“You’re not getting adopted by the Avatar because you slept in his library,” Toph said.\n\nMei stared.\n\nAang choked.\n\nToph continued. “You get choices. Adults get rules. Nobody gets to turn one bad week into your whole life.”\n\nMei’s shoulders lowered.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nLater, Aang whispered, “There were gentler ways to say that.”\n\nToph shrugged. “Mine worked.”\n\nIt had.\n\nMei’s uncle recovered enough to leave the charity ward after four weeks. He loved her. He was also still unable to work and terrified that admitting this would make the city take her away permanently.\n\nSo we did something less dramatic.\n\nWe built support.\n\nThe school offered Mei a scholarship and dorm space during the week.\n\nThe labor coalition found her uncle light-duty work at a warehouse.\n\nKatara arranged physical therapy.\n\nI found a housing subsidy nobody used because the application instructions had apparently been written by a vengeful spirit.\n\nToph hired Mei two afternoons a week to organize metal stock.\n\n“Isn’t that nepotism?” I asked.\n\n“She’s not related to me.”\n\n“Favoritism.”\n\n“She alphabetizes.”\n\n“So do I.”\n\n“You’re expensive.”\n\nAang taught Mei meditation.\n\nShe hated it.\n\nHe was delighted.\n\nOne evening she ate dinner at our house after helping at the academy.\n\nShe sat in Toph’s usual chair by accident.\n\nNobody told her.\n\nToph sat on the floor instead and stole noodles from Mei’s bowl.\n\n“That’s theft,” Mei said.\n\n“Call the Avatar.”\n\nAang raised one hand from across the table. “I’m off duty.”\n\nMei looked at me.\n\n“Are they always like this?”\n\n“Worse.”\n\nKatara smiled. “Eat.”\n\nFor a second, the kitchen held a shape I had imagined in abstract during the children conversation.\n\nA young person at the table.\n\nFour adults around them.\n\nNot ours.\n\nNot a trial run.\n\nNot evidence of what we should want.\n\nJust Mei.\n\nThat distinction saved the moment from becoming prophecy.\n\nAfter she left, Aang washed dishes.\n\nKatara dried.\n\nToph sat on the counter kicking her heels against the cabinets.\n\nI put leftovers away.\n\nAang said, “I liked that.”\n\nToph groaned. “Dangerous sentence.”\n\n“I mean having her here.”\n\nKatara looked at him.\n\n“So did I.”\n\nToph’s heels stopped.\n\nI waited.\n\nShe said, “She’s less annoying than most teenagers.”\n\nHigh praise.\n\nAang looked at me.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“I liked it too.”\n\nNobody asked what that meant.\n\nGood.\n\nA week later, the cultural school started a mutual-aid fund for students and families in unstable housing.\n\nThe first proposal was to put Aang's name across the top of the fund. The teachers believed it would attract donations. I understood the impulse. I also understood that a scholarship named for the Avatar could quietly become a test of whether a family's needs looked enough like his story.\n\nKatara read the draft and tapped the line about eligibility.\n\n“Why does this ask whether the student has an Air Nomad ancestor?”\n\nAang looked up sharply. “I didn't put that there.”\n\n“The council clerk did. They thought it made the form more authentic.”\n\nToph was leaning against the windowsill, carving a potato with a knife she insisted was a perfectly legitimate sculpture tool.\n\n“Congratulations,” she said. “You've invented a way to turn a school into a family-tree inspection.”\n\nAang took the page, crossed out the question and gave it to the teachers to rewrite. The fund would support the school community's actual housing and learning needs, not rank children by an ancestry nobody had the right to demand they prove.\n\nI had been keeping a list of everyone we were helping. It felt reassuring to watch it grow. Katara asked whether the families had agreed to have their names kept on my personal desk.\n\nI stared at the paper. Then I put it in the locked office file where the authorized staff could manage it.\n\n“We don't have to become parents to everyone to care about them,” she said quietly.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\nNot fully. I was still learning. Aang sat beside me until I could admit that without turning the admission into a request for somebody else to make it better.\n\nThe idea came from Mei.\n\nShe objected to the name.\n\n“Sounds like charity.”\n\n“What would you call it?” I asked.\n\nShe thought.\n\n“People who need stuff getting stuff.”\n\nToph slapped the table. “Perfect.”\n\nThe official name remained Mutual Aid Fund.\n\nThe unofficial ledger heading became PEOPLE WHO NEED STUFF GETTING STUFF.\n\nI did not authorize that.\n\nIt stayed.\n\nMei’s presence changed us in smaller ways.\n\nAang became aware that his school rules assumed every student had a safe home to return to.\n\nKatara noticed the clinic’s youth forms required a parent signature even for basic non-emergency care.\n\nToph realized her apprenticeship schedule excluded anyone who needed paid work during the day.\n\nI realized half the city’s family policies defined family as blood, marriage, or legal guardianship and ignored nearly every household I actually knew.\n\nSo we changed what we could.\n\nSlowly.\n\nThe future did not arrive as a baby in our arms.\n\nIt arrived as policy language, extra bowls, dorm keys, school schedules, a teenager rolling her eyes at the Avatar, and an uncle crying quietly when the housing subsidy was approved.\n\nMonths earlier, the reporter had asked which of us would bear the future Air Nation.\n\nThe question seemed even smaller now.\n\nA future was not something one body bore alone.\n\nCultures survived through teaching, practice, memory, adaptation, and people choosing to care.\n\nFamilies grew through birth sometimes.\n\nSometimes through marriage.\n\nSometimes through children who slept in archives because they had nowhere else to go.\n\nSometimes they did not grow at all, and that could still be a complete life.\n\nOne night, Mei left her knife on our kitchen table.\n\nNot forgotten.\n\nPlaced.\n\nToph noticed first.\n\nShe picked it up, tested the cheap metal with one thumb, and said nothing.\n\nThe next afternoon, she returned it with a new sheath and a reinforced handle.\n\nMei stared at it.\n\n“Why?”\n\nToph shrugged.\n\n“Your old grip was garbage.”\n\nMei hugged her.\n\nToph froze as if struck by lightning.\n\nAang turned away to hide his smile.\n\nKatara did not even try.\n\nI watched Toph awkwardly pat Mei’s back once.\n\nThen twice.\n\n“Okay,” Toph said. “That’s enough emotions.”\n\nMei released her.\n\nToph’s face was red.\n\nNobody commented.\n\nWe were learning.\n\nA future we chose would not be chosen once.\n\nIt would be built from decisions small enough to miss if we were waiting only for destiny.\n\nThe next decision was smaller.\n\nAang wanted to spend two months traveling between old Air Temple sites with a group of archivists. Katara had been invited south to help establish a permanent healer-training program. Toph’s academy was adding another class. My charter work was entering hearings again.\n\nFour schedules met on the table and immediately declared war.\n\n“Romantic,” Toph said.\n\nKatara circled dates. “We can overlap here.”\n\nAang leaned over the calendar. “I can return early from the Western Temple.”\n\n“Do you want to?” I asked.\n\nHe stopped.\n\nThe question had become a household reflex.\n\n“I want both.”\n\n“Annoying,” Toph said. “Pick a better universe.”\n\nWe laughed.\n\nEventually we stopped trying to force the next season into a single shared route.\n\nKatara would go south first. Aang would leave two weeks later. Toph and I would remain in Cranefish Town for the first month, then I would join Katara for part of the healer program while Toph traveled to an academy exhibition she pretended not to be excited about.\n\nWe would miss one another.\n\nNobody treated that as evidence the plan was wrong.\n\nAang added tiny arrows to the calendar showing where everyone would be.\n\nToph felt them with her fingertips.\n\n“Looks like a spider died on this.”\n\n“You cannot see it.”\n\n“I can sense ugliness.”\n\nKatara folded the calendar carefully.\n\nOur future did not look like four people walking permanently shoulder to shoulder.\n\nIt looked like paths crossing, separating, and crossing again because each person remained allowed to have a direction.\n\nWe copied the travel plan onto four smaller cards. Each person kept one.\n\nNot because we feared forgetting where the others were. Because it felt good to know when a letter might arrive, which moon would rise over which roof, when Appa’s shadow might appear over the street again.\n\nDistance stopped being the opposite of commitment once we gave it shape.\n\nBefore the first departure, we ate breakfast together far too early. Nobody made a speech. Aang checked his pack four times. Katara slipped extra medicine into mine. Toph complained that everyone was acting like travel had just been invented.\n\nThen we left in different directions.\n\nThe house stayed ours even while emptying.\n\nDistance became something we could plan around instead of something we had to fear.\n\nWe trusted ourselves to return.\n\nAnd we did, one journey at a time.\n\nWe chose the return as deliberately as the leaving.\n","word_count":1779}}};for(const s of lib.series){const map=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=map[ch.id];if(e&&ch.chapter_number===e.number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}}})();
